  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nA slow moving low pressure system will continue to track \neastward overhead into tonight, sending a cold front through \neastern New York and western New England. Ahead of the boundary,\nwe remain this afternoon within the warm sector where \ntemperatures have largely reached the mid/upper 70s to low 80s \nwith dewpoints in the upper 60s. Higher moisture content has \nseen surface-based instability rise to around around 1000-1500 \nJ/kg, according to the latest SPC Mesoscale Analysis. However, \nwith the atmosphere having been significantly worked over during\nlast night's upstream severe convection; an overall lack of \nsubstantial forcing; and increasing subsidence as the convergent\nzone of an anticyclonic jet approaches, that instability is \ngoing to largely go to waste. Little in the way of convective \nactivity is expected with the aforementioned front this \nafternoon, outside of isolated showers and perhaps a non- severe\nthunderstorm or two south of I-90. \n\nIn fact, much of the next several days will feature little in the \nway of precipitation. Persistent cyclonic flow about the departing \nupper-level cyclone associated with the surface low will ensure \ndecreased moisture can be overcome with upslope flow in the \nAdirondacks and Southern Greens. Isolated to scattered light showers \ncan therefore be expected in these areas at times over the next \ncouple of days, but particularly on Saturday when a shortwave pulses \naround the southern periphery of the upper-level system. By Sunday, \nregionwide dry conditions return and persist through Monday as high \npressure builds in and remains steady. In fact, Monday, Labor Day, \nwill be absolutely beautiful with highs in the 70s and clear skies. \nChances for precipitation won't widely increase again until the \nmiddle to end of next week when our next frontal system is\nexpected.",
